// c-data-map-02: `{(e) with f: v}` refuses a source typed by a typedef name
// (or by an uncollapsed union) that `(e).f` accepts.
//
// command: graphix --check design/review-2026-10-05/repro/c-data-map-02.gx
//          graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/c-data-map-02.gx
//
// expected: accepted; prints {x: 3, y: 2}, as `let p = w.p; {p with x: 3.0}`
// does (Point is {x: f64, y: f64}, structural typing).
//
// observed (HEAD c722befe, debug build): exit 1, at { (w.p) with x: 3.0 }:
//   expected a struct
// while `(w.p).x` on the same w prints 1.
//
// StructWith::typecheck0_with (graphix-compiler/src/node/data.rs:1052) reads
// its source with deref_cloned, which follows bound cells only. `w.p` is
// typed Type::Ref(Point) (the field's declared type), so it falls into
// `_ => bail!("expected a struct")`. StructRef uses struct_field_type
// (data.rs:852), whose deref_typ! expands refs and normalizes sets. A name
// works only because a pattern bind expands a top-level ref
// (pattern.rs compile_int_inner). Refused the same way:
//   {(t.0) with x: 3.0} with t: (Point, i64);
//   the nested update |s: State| -> State {s with cursor: {(s.cursor) with
//     row: s.cursor.row + 1}} with type State = {cursor: Cursor, name: string};
//   an uncollapsed union source:
//     let k = 1; let n = never();
//     let s = select k { 0 => {x: n, y: 2}, _ => {x: 3, y: 4} };
//     n <- 7; {s with x: 5}
//   is refused with "expected a struct", while `s.x` in its place prints 3.
type Point = {x: f64, y: f64};
let w: {p: Point, n: i64} = {p: {x: 1.0, y: 2.0}, n: 0};
{(w.p) with x: 3.0}
